# DESCRIPTION

The functions follow POSIX and return 0 on success or an error number; none sets `errno`.
Threads are croi's threads, and the locks are built on futexes.
The layouts of the types are the library's own, and an all-zero object is a default one: `PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER`, `PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER` and `PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER` are zeros, and `PTHREAD_ONCE_INIT` is 0.

## Threads

`pthread_create` starts a thread running `start(arg)` and stores its identifier in <thread>.
A `pthread_t` is the address of the thread's record.
The thread ends when <start> returns, its value being the result, or when it calls `pthread_exit`; its thread-specific data destructors then run.
<attr> may be null.
Creation fails with `EAGAIN` if there is no memory or no thread can be made.

`pthread_join` waits for the thread to end, stores its result in <result> if that is not null, and frees the thread.
It fails with `ESRCH` for a null identifier, `EDEADLK` for the calling thread itself, and `EINVAL` for a detached thread and for a thread that `pthread_create` did not start (the main thread, or one made by Sys).
A thread is joined once; a second join uses freed memory.

`pthread_detach` lets a thread free itself when it ends, or frees it now if it has ended; `EINVAL` if it is already detached or was not started by `pthread_create`.
`pthread_self` returns the calling thread's identifier; a thread `pthread_create` did not start gets one the first time it asks.
`pthread_equal` returns 1 if the identifiers are equal and 0 if not.

An attribute object holds the detach state (`PTHREAD_CREATE_JOINABLE`, the default, or `PTHREAD_CREATE_DETACHED`; `EINVAL` otherwise) and a stack size.
`pthread_attr_setstacksize` fails with `EINVAL` below 16384 bytes.
The size is only recorded: every thread has a stack of the same size, 256 KiB, and `pthread_attr_getstacksize` returns 256 KiB for an object that was not set.

## Mutexes

The types, `PTHREAD_MUTEX_NORMAL` (0, the default), `PTHREAD_MUTEX_RECURSIVE` (1) and `PTHREAD_MUTEX_ERRORCHECK` (2), are set in an attribute object with `pthread_mutexattr_settype`, which fails with `EINVAL` for any other number.
`pthread_mutex_lock` sleeps until the mutex is free.
Locking an error-checking mutex the thread holds fails with `EDEADLK`; a recursive mutex counts the locks (`EAGAIN` if the count would overflow).
A normal mutex locked twice by its owner deadlocks.
`pthread_mutex_trylock` fails with `EBUSY` instead of waiting.
`pthread_mutex_timedlock` waits until <abstime>, an absolute time on `CLOCK_REALTIME`, and fails with `ETIMEDOUT`, or with `EINVAL` if the nanoseconds are out of range.
`pthread_mutex_unlock` of a recursive or error-checking mutex the thread does not hold fails with `EPERM`; of a normal mutex it is not checked.
`pthread_mutex_destroy` fails with `EBUSY` if the mutex is locked.

## Condition variables

`pthread_cond_wait` unlocks <mutex>, sleeps until signaled and locks it again.
`pthread_cond_timedwait` also returns `ETIMEDOUT` at <abstime>, on the clock of the variable's attribute: `CLOCK_REALTIME` by default, or `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` set with `pthread_condattr_setclock` (any other clock is `EINVAL`).
A wait may return without a signal, as POSIX allows.
`pthread_cond_signal` wakes one waiter and `pthread_cond_broadcast` all; with no waiter they cost no system call.
`pthread_cond_destroy` fails with `EBUSY` while threads wait.
A recursive or error-checking mutex given to a wait that the thread does not hold fails with `EPERM`; for a normal mutex ownership is not checked.

## Read-write locks

Any number of readers, or one writer, hold a lock.
`pthread_rwlock_rdlock` and `pthread_rwlock_wrlock` sleep until they can; `pthread_rwlock_tryrdlock` and `pthread_rwlock_trywrlock` fail with `EBUSY` instead.
There is no preference between readers and writers: whoever gets there first.
A read lock taken more than 2 to the 31 less 1 times fails with `EAGAIN`.
`pthread_rwlock_unlock` fails with `EPERM` on a lock nobody holds; it does not check who holds it.
`pthread_rwlock_destroy` fails with `EBUSY` if the lock is held.
<attr> is accepted and ignored.

## Once and thread-specific data

`pthread_once` runs <init> the first time it is called with <once>, and a thread that finds another running it sleeps until it is done.
The routine must not call `pthread_once` on the same <once>.

There are `PTHREAD_KEYS_MAX` (128) keys.
`pthread_key_create` makes one, with an optional <destructor>, and fails with `EAGAIN` when all are in use.
A value is a thread's own, null in each until set with `pthread_setspecific`.
At thread exit the destructor of each key with a non-null value is called with the value, repeating up to `PTHREAD_DESTRUCTOR_ITERATIONS` (4) rounds while values remain.
`pthread_key_delete` frees the key and runs no destructor; values set under a deleted key never show through a key made in its place.
`pthread_setspecific` and `pthread_key_delete` of a key not in use fail with `EINVAL`.

